      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to determine when the expression is non-negative. This involves analyzing the numerator and the denominator separately.\n\n1. **Analyze the numerator:**\n   The numerator is \\(x + 6\\). The expression \\(x + 6\\) is zero when \\(x = -6\\). This means \\(x = -6\\) is a critical point.\n\n2. **Analyze the denominator:**\n   The denominator is \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\). We need to determine if this quadratic expression changes sign. The quadratic expression \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) can be analyzed by checking its discriminant:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24.\n   \\]\n   Since the discriminant is negative, the quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) has no real roots and is always positive (since the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is positive). This means the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive for all real \\(x\\).\n\n3. **Combine the analyses:**\n   Since the denominator is always positive, the sign of the expression \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) depends solely on the sign of the numerator \\(x + 6\\). We need to determine when \\(x + 6 \\ge 0\\), because if \\(x + 6 \\ge 0\\), then \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) (since the denominator is positive and does not change the sign).\n\n   Solving the inequality \\(x + 6 \\ge 0\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   x \\ge -6.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) is \\(\\boxed{[-6, \\infty)}\\).",
