      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to analyze the sign of the expression \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\).\n\nFirst, let's identify the values of \\(x\\) that make the numerator zero:\n\\[x + 6 = 0 \\implies x = -6.\\]\n\nNext, let's consider the denominator:\n\\[x^2 + 2x + 7.\\]\nWe need to determine if this quadratic expression is always positive. A quadratic expression \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\) is always positive if its discriminant is negative and the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is positive. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is given by:\n\\[\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24.\\]\nSince the discriminant is negative, the quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) has no real roots and is always positive. Therefore, \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 > 0\\) for all real \\(x\\).\n\nGiven that the denominator is always positive, the sign of the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) depends only on the sign of the numerator \\(x + 6\\). We need to determine where \\(x + 6\\) is non-negative. The inequality \\(x + 6 \\ge 0\\) simplifies to:\n\\[x \\ge -6.\\]\n\nThus, the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is non-negative when \\(x \\ge -6\\). Since the expression is undefined at \\(x = -6\\) (which makes the denominator zero), we exclude \\(x = -6\\) from the solution set.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) is:\n\\[\\boxed{[-6, \\infty)}.\\]",
